# molecular phylogenetic analysis of canidae

## Project overview

Understanding the evolutionary relationships among organisms is an important part of studying biology. One way scientists do this is by comparing DNA sequences to determine how closely different species are related through phylogenetic analysis. The mitochondrial cytochrome c oxidase subunit I (COI/COX1) gene was used because it is a reliable genetic marker that contains enough similarities and differences to distinguish related species (Antil, S et al 2023).
COI/COX1 nucleotide sequences of selected members of the family Canidae with one outgroup were obtained from the NCBI database, aligned using the Concatenate datasets tail-to-head (cat) tool in the Galaxy platform, and analyzed to generate a phylogenetic tree. The resulting tree allowed the evolutionary relationships among the selected species to be examined and compared with findings from published scientific studies.


